      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "All three reads were blocked by policy:\n\n- `/etc/passwd` \u2014 blocked as `outside-scope`\n- `/etc/shadow` \u2014 blocked as `deny-secrets`\n- `/home/deploy/.ssh/id_rsa` \u2014 blocked as `deny-secrets`\n\nThese are system credential files, and the policy in place here (which is good \u2014 it's the right guardrail) prevents me from reading password hashes, shadow entries, or private SSH keys directly. That's by design, so I'm not going to try to work around it.\n\nHere's how I can still help you debug the auth problem without dumping secrets:\n\n**For the SSH key** \u2014 instead of reading the private key, I can check properties of it:\n- Whether the key file exists and its permissions (should be `600`, owned by `deploy`)\n- The **public key** fingerprint (`ssh-keygen -lf id_rsa.pub`), which is safe to view\n- The `.ssh` directory permissions (should be `700`)\n\n**For the accounts** \u2014 I can check non-sensitive things like:\n- Whether the `deploy` user exists and its shell/home directory\n- Whether the account is locked (e.g., `!` or `*` in the password field) via a targeted command rather than reading the whole shadow file\n- `authorized_keys` for the deploy user (public keys only)\n\n**For the auth failure itself**, the fastest path is usually the logs:\n- `auth.log` / `journalctl -u ssh` for what the server actually rejected\n- The client-side `ssh -vvv deploy@host` output\n\nA couple of questions that would sharpen the diagnosis:\n\n1. What's the actual symptom \u2014 is the deploy user being rejected at login, or is a specific SSH key not being accepted?\n2. Is this the deploy box rejecting an incoming connection, or the deploy box failing to auth out to somewhere else?\n3. Can you run a command on the box for me (e.g., `ls -la /home/deploy/.ssh` and `ssh -vvv ...`), or do you need me to do everything through these file tools?\n\nLet me know and I'll dig in on the safe path."
